<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emilio De Bono</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emilio De Bono was an Italian general and one of the four leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-rome">March on Rome</a> in 1922. He served as governor of Tripolitania and minister of the colonies, and in October 1935 commanded the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-badoglio">Pietro Badoglio</a> replaced him in November. In July 1943 he voted against Mussolini in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-of-fascism">Fascist Grand Council</a>, and he was tried and shot at Verona by the Italian Social Republic in January 1944.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Emilio De Bono was born in Cassano d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a>, the son of Giovanni de Bono and Elisa Bazzi. He entered the Royal Italian Army as a second lieutenant in 1884 and fought in the Italo-Ethiopian War of 1887-1889. By the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a> in 1911 he had reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>, and he received the Knight's Cross of the Military Order of Savoy for his conduct there. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> on the Karst Plateau, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piave">Piave</a> and on Monte Grappa, and won three Silver Medals of Military Valour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the early 1920s De Bono helped organise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-fascist-party">National Fascist Party</a> and, as one of the four Quadrumvirs, organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-rome">March on Rome</a>. Afterwards he served as Chief of Police and Commander of the Fascist Militia. He was tried in 1925 over the death of the leftist politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-matteotti">Giacomo Matteotti</a>, refused to implicate his superiors and was acquitted. Later that year he became governor of Tripolitania, where he was a key figure in anti-partisan policies, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>, that killed tens of thousands of civilians and have been described as genocidal. In 1929 he became Minister of Colonial Affairs.</p><p>In November 1932 De Bono wrote, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s request, a plan for an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> that was to be slow, cheap and safe. The army drew up a far larger plan of its own, and in 1935 De Bono became supreme commander of the operation. His forces crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> on 3 October, took Adowa on 6 October and captured Mek'ele on 8 November. He was made Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on 16 November, but Mussolini, impatient at the slow advance, relieved him in December and replaced him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-badoglio">Pietro Badoglio</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1940 De Bono commanded a defence corps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. On 24 and 25 July 1943 he was among the members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-of-fascism">Grand Council of Fascism</a> who voted to oust <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>. After Mussolini was freed by German forces and set up the Italian Social Republic, De Bono and others who had voted against him were tried for treason at Verona. He was shot on 11 January 1944, aged 77, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-ciano">Galeazzo Ciano</a>, Luciano Gottardi, Giovanni Marinelli and Carlo Pareschi, and complained of being shot in the back as a traitor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emilio De Bono” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Second Italo-Ethiopian War of 1935–1936, led by <b>Emilio De Bono</b> and then Pietro Badoglio, ended in the conquest of Ethiopia through numerical superiority, mechanised forces, aerial bombardment and the use of chemical weapons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cassano d’Adda, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Verona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.53,9.52&amp;year=1916" aria-label="Cassano d’Adda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Emilio De Bono"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5568 6183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5568 6183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.53,9.52&amp;year=1916">What was here in 1916? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
